\section{Why these pairs are the interesting ones}{

Every pair here is a difference that \strong{survived} normalization.
\link[npmatch:np_normalize]{np_normalize()} already strips a leading \code{THE},
rewrites \code{&} to \code{AND}, expands \code{FDN} to \code{FOUNDATION} and contracts USPS
street suffixes, and \code{\link[=pf_dedup]{pf_dedup()}} then collapses renderings that share an
identity key. So nothing in this table differs only in a way the pipeline
already handles: these are the cases that reached the comparator and split.
That makes the table a regression corpus. Tighten the normalizer and
specific documented pairs should move; the pairs recorded as already closed
should stay closed.
}

\section{What it does not contain}{

Ground truth. The triangulation verdicts are silver, \code{confidence = "low"},
and unadjudicated — assigning the anchor's EIN to every other rendering at
the address is the assumption under test, not a fact. \code{expected_same_org} is
therefore \code{TRUE} on only 20 rows, where the two names are the same string
under a documented cosmetic transformation at an identical address, and \code{NA}
— never \code{FALSE} — on the other 5,390. A pair sharing no tokens may still be
one organization (\verb{US OLYMPIC AND PARALYMPIC FOUNDATION} / \code{USOC}); a pair
sharing a distinctive token may still be two (\verb{MARINE CORPS UNIVERSITY FOUNDATION} / \verb{MARINE CORP SCHOLARSHIP FUND}). \code{label_basis} records which
rows are waiting for a human.

Pairs where either side is a person rather than an organization are
excluded, so the table carries no individual's name or home address.
